===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*īwaz}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*h₁eyH-weh₂-}}, a suffixed form of the base {{m|ine-pro|*h₁eyH-||reddish, colourful}}. Cognate with Old High German {{m|goh|īwa}} (German {{m|de|Eibe}}), Middle Low German {{m|gml|īwe}}, Dutch {{m|nl|ijf}}, Old Norse {{m|non|ýr||bow, yew}}. The Indo-European root is perhaps also the source of Old Irish {{m|sga|ibar}} (Irish {{m|ga|iúr}}), Proto-Balto-Slavic {{m|ine-bsl-pro|*eiwas}} (Lithuanian {{m|lt|ievà}}), Proto-Slavic {{m|sla-pro|*jьva}} (Russian {{m|ru|ива||willow}}), Latin {{m|la|ūva||bunch of grapes}}).

The West Germanic languages have a variant {{m|gem-pro|*īhwaz}} with a guttural consonant, represented by Old English {{m|ang|ēoh}}.

